Interfaces: who provides each, who requires it, and what its contract says
(#612, FJ4).

A zk2 interface is a contract, `<name>.v<major>`, and each revision of it is a
fingerprint (spec §9). Providers come from interface tokens and, for the
tokenless set (U22), from descriptors alone — which is why `iface show` reads
the whole namespace's presence rather than the interface's tokens. Consumers
come from the roles descriptors declare (R3). Contracts come from
`--contracts` (authoring files or a `.history` root) or are retrieved from
their holders by fingerprint (§8.4). They replaced v1's `interface` (a payload
type across producers) and `topic` (what a registry declares).

```console
$ zenctl iface --help
Interfaces: who provides each, who requires it, and its contract.

A zk2 interface is a contract, `<name>.v<major>`, and each revision of it is a fingerprint (spec §9). Providers come from interface tokens and, for the tokenless set, from descriptors; consumers from the roles descriptors declare (R3); contracts from `--contracts` or retrieved from their holders by fingerprint (§8.4).

Usage: zenctl iface <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list  Every interface provided or required, and by whom
  show  One interface: providers, consumers, and each revision's contract
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl iface list --help
Every interface provided or required, and by whom.

From one presence read and every instance's descriptor: providers by interface token or, for the tokenless set, by descriptor; consumers by the roles descriptors declare; the revisions providers name. A read that ran to its timeout is possibly incomplete, and says so.

Usage: zenctl iface list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl iface show --help
One interface: providers, consumers, and each revision's contract.

Each provider with its token and its descriptor's fingerprint, the resources it exposes by the compact rule (spec §3.3) when its revision is in hand, its unavailable list and cardinality bounds; each role bound to the interface; and each revision's contract — resources, kinds, QoS, fan-out, types — from `--contracts` or retrieved from its holders (spec §8.4). `@<fingerprint>` narrows the view to one revision, and shows its contract even when no provider names it now. Exit 2 when nothing provides, requires or describes the interface: silence is not an answer.

Usage: zenctl iface show [OPTIONS] <IFACE[@FP]>

Arguments:
  <IFACE[@FP]>
          `<name>.v<major>`, optionally `@<fingerprint>` (or a prefix of one)

Options:
      --contracts <PATH>
          Contracts known offline, repeatable: an authoring file (`*.toml`), a directory of them, or a `.history` root.
          
          They seed the contract store, so a revision held here is never retrieved from the bus (spec §8.5) — and a question they answer alone opens no session at all. A file that is not a valid contract is reported on stderr; a path that loads nothing is refused (exit 2).

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```
